From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sam Attard <samuel.r.attard@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 11:11:41 -0700
Subject: [CSS] Run the marking barrier when switching a CSSSelector to
 RareData

CreateRareData() and SetSelectorList() switch the active DataUnion
member by placement-new'ing a Member from the new pointer. Member's
initializing constructor skips the marking write barrier, which is only
sound for a freshly constructed object, but the parser's :host:has()
fix-up performs this switch on a selector that already lives in its
(possibly already traced) arena. Construct the Member empty and assign
instead, so the barrier runs.

Bug: 574409610
Change-Id: I4c9a319986a7e149c4f168561fab17fae580c212
Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/c/chromium/src/+/8600147
Reviewed-by: Rune Lillesveen <futhark@chromium.org>
Reviewed-by: Daniil Sakhapov <sakhapov@chromium.org>
Commit-Queue: Samuel Attard <samuel.r.attard@gmail.com>
Cr-Commit-Position: refs/heads/main@{#1722127}

Electron adaptation notes (43-x-y): This branch has no inline CSSSelectorList storage (HasInlineSelectorList / SetSelectorList inline path), so only the CreateRareData() value_ -> rare_data_ transition is changed to placement-new an empty Member and assign it (running the marking write barrier). css_selector_test.cc is test-only and conflicted; it is not carried.

diff --git a/third_party/blink/renderer/core/css/css_selector.cc b/third_party/blink/renderer/core/css/css_selector.cc
index 6cd5fa2dddf86dfa388523a229d8cfcf55a8f8f2..f1be900d83d1ef108d8afdf7445a1f2704fe830a 100644
--- a/third_party/blink/renderer/core/css/css_selector.cc
+++ b/third_party/blink/renderer/core/css/css_selector.cc
@@ -153,9 +153,18 @@ void CSSSelector::CreateRareData() {
   // to be careful to correctly manage explicitly destruction of |value_|
   // followed by placement new of |rare_data_|. A straight-assignment will
   // compile and may kinda work, but will be undefined behavior.
+  //
+  // The new Member is placement-new'ed empty and then *assigned*, rather
+  // than constructed from the pointer: Member's initializing constructor
+  // deliberately skips the marking write barrier (a freshly constructed
+  // object is always white), but this CSSSelector may already live inside a
+  // HeapVector backing that incremental marking has traced (the parser
+  // mutates selectors in its arena in place), in which case only the
+  // assigning barrier gets the new RareData marked.
   auto* rare_data = MakeGarbageCollected<RareData>(data_.value_);
   data_.value_.~AtomicString();
-  new (&data_.rare_data_) Member<RareData>(rare_data);
+  new (&data_.rare_data_) Member<RareData>();
+  data_.rare_data_ = rare_data;
   bits_.set<HasRareDataField>(true);
 }
 
